labor secretary Eugene Scalia he joins  us live from the DC area good morning to  you mr. secretary good morning good to  be with you you know yesterday I spoke  on the phone with Kansas labor secretary  Garcia and you know this strikes  everybody her family down Wichita has  had a restaurant there for decades  they're closed everything you know  everybody is on unemployment the state  of Kansas is you know has a very low  rate of unemployment on a regular week  maybe 1300 would apply over the last  week 55,000 apply they had to quadruple  the the call center it is all hands on  deck at the state level as well as the  federal level to make sure that the  people who file unemployment can get  their money and ultimately get the  stimulus as well right yes the states  are working very hard to get these  payments out as you know one of the key  provisions in the cares Act which the  President signed two weeks ago was this  enhancement to unemployment compensation  benefits there that are ordinary  ordinarily made available by the states  it's additional $600 a week that's being  made available and the states are  working very hard to to make that money  available I think now seven states are  making that available to people who've  lost work as well as 2gig economy  workers independent contractors aren't  ordinarily eligible for unemployment  compensation but this legislation that  the president moved through Congress and  does make it available for them to mr.  secretary talked about the bounce-back  we heard the president yesterday saying  we will the economy will turn around do  you agree with him and how are we gonna  do that what will I do agree and I share  that optimism these are such challenging  times for workers and their families and  we recognize that the program that we  were just talking about is one  reflection of the efforts here in  Washington to address it but at the same  time I'm mindful of just how strong our  economy was five weeks ago you know at  the start of March we put out one of the  strongest jobs reports we've seen in  this country in 50 years  that's where we were and that's where we  want to get back to as I mentioned last  night when I spoke at the briefing we  had a blue-collar boom and and now we  want a blue-collar bounce back and I  think that we still have the  fundamentals there our economy is  hurting now not because of an underlying  weakness in the economy but because of a  health emergency so we need to tackle  that and you have been so good at urging  your viewers to take these measures to  beat the virus but then we do want to  get back to work  and I think we have the foundation the  fundamental and the presidential  policies in place to be optimistic about  that about that recovery so mr.  secretary we see Denmark begin to get it  back online  Austria get back online Germany get back  online Taiwan is back online  Singapore's back online are you studying  those countries to see how to do it even  though we're so much bigger and with  such diverse such diversity are you  where she would model are you looking at  on for Americans getting back to work  well we're certainly looking at what  other countries have done and we're  looking at what this country has done in  the past too you know we look back 100  years ago  dealing with the Spanish flu there  lessons to be learned there we know a  couple of things first that discipline  right now remains extremely important we  know that we will need to get back  before this virus is entirely gone from  among us and we have to plan for that we  know it might not be the same date in  every location that we're we're all back  to work we'll need to plan accordingly  but we also know that we can't wait too  long we want to bounce back that does  get harder the longer that that people  are kept from work and we have to keep  that in the balances were absolutely  sure and as labor secretary you know you  know that a lot of people want to get  back to work as quickly as possible  and regarding that story in The  Washington Post this morning that  apparently the administration the  president in particular would like to  have essentially American business  reopened  maybe in stages by May 1st but there are  going to be hot spots it can't  one-size-fits-all yep we're open for  business again can you explain as much  as you can about what's going on behind  the scenes to get us to that place well  obviously there are a number of  different components to this that the  health component and Public Health  Policy is is critical to get right the  measures that have been put in place to  tide workers over are so important to  them individually the Paycheck  protection program which you talked  about a moment ago for a small business  is one of the things in this recent  legislation that I like most because not  only does it provide income to workers  during this difficult time it keeps them  tied to their employer so that when  we're ready to reopen that relationship  still exists that that's something we've  been talking about obviously there's an  interest in extending that program and  then we're talking about the health and  welfare of people when they return to  work that is something that we're going  to have to look after it's important to  us now at the Labor Department and as we  reopen we're going to want to make sure  that it's an environment that's safe and  healthy mr. secretary there was a  headline this morning saying some folks  some Americans are gonna get those  stimulus checks today can you update us  on on when people will get that money  well there are a couple of different  streams of relief coming first there is  as I just mentioned the Paycheck  protection program and there's more than  I think 140 billion in loans already  approved through that program and those  loans are now being made available to  small businesses right and that will  continue over time then we have the  payments coming from 